# Routes the previous multi-page site served, pointed at the part of the single
# page that absorbed them. Everything here 404s otherwise: the rebuild in
# e32628c dropped the pages, and the assets-only Worker has no route left to
# match, so a bookmark or a search result from before the rebuild dead-ends.
#
# 301 rather than 302 on all of them — these moved for good, and a permanent
# redirect is what tells a crawler to replace the old URL with the new one
# instead of keeping both.
#
# Each path is listed twice: bare, and with a `/*` splat. The splat is what
# catches the trailing slash the old build format emitted (`/about/`), and for
# the collections it also catches their entries and their paginated pages, all of
# which now live inside the section they belonged to.
#
# Cloudflare's limit is 100 redirects with a splat or a placeholder in them —
# these are dynamic rules under that rule, so keep the list short by widening a
# pattern rather than by enumerating slugs.

# The three standalone pages. `/terms` had no content worth keeping; the home
# page is a better answer to it than a 404.
/about        /#research      301
/about/*      /#research      301
/contact      /#contact       301
/contact/*    /#contact       301
/terms        /               301
/terms/*      /               301

# The collections. Publications kept their section; projects were folded into
# the interests cards, which is the nearest thing the page still has to them.
/publications    /#publications  301
/publications/*  /#publications  301
/projects        /#interests     301
/projects/*      /#interests     301

# The blog, its tag index, and its feed. Nothing replaced them, so they go to the
# top of the page rather than to a section that would only half-answer the link.
/blog       /  301
/blog/*     /  301
/tags       /  301
/tags/*     /  301
/rss.xml    /  301
